  "handoff": {
    "summary": "Pinned 261 Sage/PARI raw and canonical-completed values at 53, 100, and 200 bits for ten curve models, plus 20 independent 70-decimal-digit Magma comparisons. The one-process capture and verification harnesses record exact curve, coefficient-prefix, normalization, precision, and source metadata. The corpus checker covers stable precision prefixes, conjugation, the functional equation, trivial and near-trivial-zero behavior, ranks 0 through 5, both root signs, and isomorphic integral/nonintegral models. Matched Sage/Sage.js and Magma benchmark scaffolding separates setup, cold, repeated, independent, and native batch work.",
    "risks": [
      "All values are non-rigorous numerical approximations, and high analytic-rank labels are classifications rather than proofs.",
      "PARI lfunlambda for elliptic gammaV=[0,1] is twice the project's canonical completion; the harness deliberately divides it by two and the provenance/checker freeze that convention.",
      "The installed old Magma is prohibitively slow for the conductor-430250329 curve at 70 decimal digits, so independent Magma fixtures stop at conductor 1008811 while Sage/PARI covers the large public example."
    ],
    "next_steps": [
      "Wire test/elliptic-lseries-oracles.cjs into the integration lane's focused public test command without changing the offline fixture schema.",
      "Use raw and completed corpus records directly in reference/native differential tests, including s=-1+2^-20 to prevent trivial-zero snapping.",
      "Run benchmark.sage under the completed Sage.js API on bench-1 and compare native batch time against the retained independent-point Sage baseline."
    ]
  }
